"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[89348],{566967:e=>{e.exports='# Init Scripts\n\nThis file covers the full init-script translation pipeline — finding init scripts in a classic job config, fetching and categorizing their contents, deciding whether translation can proceed, and extracting any library installations into a `requirements.txt` referenced from the serverless job\'s `environments` spec.\n\nThe pipeline has two phases connected by a plan. **Phase 1** (Steps 1–6) is read-only: discover, fetch, categorize, parse, and produce a translation plan. **Phase 2** (Steps 7–8) writes files and updates the job config using the plan.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Analyze\n\n## Step 1: Find Init Scripts\n\nInit scripts are referenced in the job config under the cluster definition. Use `runDatabricksCli` → `jobs get <job_id> --output JSON` and look for init scripts in these locations:\n\n### In `new_cluster` (task-level or job cluster)\n\n```json\n{\n  "job_clusters": [\n    {\n      "job_cluster_key": "my_cluster",\n      "new_cluster": {\n        "init_scripts": [\n          { "workspace": { "destination": "/Shared/team-scripts/setup.sh" } },\n          { "dbfs": { "destination": "dbfs:/cluster-init/install-libs.sh" } },\n          { "s3": { "destination": "s3://bucket/scripts/init.sh" } },\n          { "volumes": { "destination": "/Volumes/catalog/schema/vol/init.sh" } },\n          { "gcs": { "destination": "gs://bucket/init.sh" } },\n          { "abfss": { "destination": "abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/init.sh" } }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### In cluster policies\n\nIf the job references a `policy_id`, the cluster policy may inject init scripts via fixed values. Use `runDatabricksCli` → `cluster-policies get <policy_id> --output JSON` to check for `init_scripts.*` rules.\n\n### Collect all init scripts\n\nBuild a complete list of init scripts from both the direct cluster config and any referenced cluster policy. Each entry has a **storage type** and a **destination path**.\n\n**Path format**: Workspace paths from the Jobs API do not include a `/Workspace` prefix (e.g., `/Shared/init-scripts/setup.sh`). When accessing files on the filesystem, prepend `/Workspace` to get the full path. Without this prefix, `open()` will fail.\n\n## Step 2: Fetch Init Script Contents\n\nHow you fetch the contents depends on the storage type:\n\n| Storage Type   | Key in JSON             | Fetchable? | How to Fetch                                                        |\n| -------------- | ----------------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Workspace**  | `workspace.destination` | Yes        | Use `readAssetById` (`assetType: \'file\'`) with the destination path |\n| **UC Volumes** | `volumes.destination`   | Yes        | Use `runDatabricksCli`: `fs cat <destination>`                      |\n| **DBFS**       | `dbfs.destination`      | Yes        | Use `runDatabricksCli`: `fs cat <destination>`                      |\n| **S3**         | `s3.destination`        | **No**     | BLOCKED — ask user to copy to workspace or provide contents         |\n| **GCS**        | `gcs.destination`       | **No**     | BLOCKED — ask user to copy to workspace or provide contents         |\n| **ABFSS**      | `abfss.destination`     | **No**     | BLOCKED — ask user to copy to workspace or provide contents         |\n| **Local file** | `file.destination`      | **No**     | BLOCKED — ask user to copy to workspace or provide contents         |\n\nWhen a script is unfetchable, report to the user:\n\n> "Init script at `<path>` is stored in cloud storage / a local file path and cannot be accessed remotely. Please either:\n>\n> 1. Copy the script to a workspace file (e.g., `/Workspace/Shared/init-scripts/`) and update the cluster config, or\n> 2. Paste the script contents into this chat so I can analyze it."\n\n## Step 3: Categorize Init Script Operations\n\nRead through each init script line by line and classify operations into categories:\n\n### Translatable — Library Installation\n\nThese patterns indicate pip/conda library installation.\n\n**pip install patterns:**\n\n- `pip install <packages...>`\n- `pip3 install <packages...>`\n- `/databricks/python3/bin/pip install <packages...>`\n- `python -m pip install <packages...>`\n- `pip install -r <path>` (requirements file reference)\n- `pip install --index-url <url> <packages...>`\n- `pip install --extra-index-url <url> <packages...>`\n\n**conda install patterns:**\n\n- `conda install <packages...>`\n- `conda install -c <channel> <packages...>`\n\n### Not Translatable — Abort Migration\n\nThese operations have no serverless equivalent. If found, **abort the entire migration** (see Step 4 Abort Policy). Report which operations triggered the abort.\n\n**System package installation:**\n\n- `apt-get install ...`\n- `yum install ...`\n- `dpkg -i ...`\n- `rpm -i ...`\n\n**System configuration:**\n\n- `sysctl ...`\n- `ulimit ...`\n- Modifying files in `/etc/`\n- `chmod`, `chown` on system paths\n- `mount` commands\n\n**Binary/tool installation:**\n\n- `wget`/`curl` downloading executables (not Python packages)\n- Installing custom binaries to `/usr/local/bin` or similar\n\n**Editable Python installs:**\n\n- `pip install -e .`, `pip install -e /path/to/project` (references a local source directory that can\'t be expressed as a requirements.txt line)\n\n**Cloud storage references in install commands:**\n\n- `pip install -r s3://...`\n- `pip install -r gs://...`\n- `pip install -r abfss://...`\n\nNote: DBFS paths (`/dbfs/...`) and Workspace paths (`/Workspace/...`) are accessible from serverless compute and do NOT trigger an abort.\n\n### Not Yet Translatable — Flag to User (Future Support)\n\nThese are recognized but not yet handled. Proceed with translating the library operations, skip these, and note to the user which operations were not translated.\n\n**Environment variables:**\n\n- `export VAR=value`\n- `VAR=value` at the script top level\n\n## Step 4: Make the Translation Decision\n\nAfter categorizing all operations in an init script:\n\n| Scenario                                         | Action                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ALL operations are translatable library installs | **Proceed** to Step 5                                                                |\n| Mix of library installs + env var exports only   | **Proceed** to Step 5, skip env vars, note to user that env vars were not translated |\n| ANY untranslatable system operations present     | **Abort entire migration** — report which operations are untranslatable              |\n| ANY cloud storage references in install commands | **Abort entire migration** — report which paths are inaccessible                     |\n| Script is empty or only comments                 | **Skip** — nothing to translate, just remove the init script reference               |\n\n### Abort Policy\n\nIf ANY init script contains untranslatable operations (system packages, custom binaries, cloud storage references, etc.), **abort the entire migration** — not just the infrastructure translation for the script that triggered it. A job\'s init scripts may have interdependencies, and partial translation risks producing a broken environment.\n\nWhen aborting, report to the user:\n\n- Which init script triggered the abort\n- Which specific operations are untranslatable\n- What the user would need to change to unblock the migration\n\n---\n\nThe remaining steps apply to each init script whose Step 4 decision was **Proceed**. Each init script produces its own `requirements.txt`; never merge libraries from multiple init scripts into a single file. This preserves traceability — one source script maps to one translated artifact.\n\n**CRITICAL**: Never place library dependencies directly in the environment spec\'s `dependencies` array. Always create a requirements.txt intermediary file and reference it via `-r "/Workspace/Shared/serverless-migration/<filename>.txt"`. This rule applies regardless of how simple the init script is — even a single `pip install` line must go through this flow.\n\n## Step 5: Parse Library Installation Commands\n\nInit scripts commonly have multiple `pip install` and/or `conda install` lines. Process every library installation command across the whole script and merge their outputs into one requirements.txt — don\'t stop at the first one.\n\n### pip install commands\n\n**Principle**: preserve anything that tells pip _what_ to install (package specs, `-r` or `-c` file references, `--index-url`, `--extra-index-url`, `--trusted-host`, wheel paths, VCS URLs, extras like `requests[security]`, environment markers like `numpy; python_version >= "3.10"`). Drop anything that controls _how_ pip runs at install time (`--upgrade`, `--force-reinstall`, `--no-cache-dir`, etc. — see the full list below). The examples below show common patterns; apply the same principle to unfamiliar arguments.\n\nExtract packages and flags from pip install lines:\n\n```bash\n# Simple installs → one line per package in requirements.txt\npip install requests numpy==1.24.0 pandas>=2.0\n# → requests\n# → numpy==1.24.0\n# → pandas>=2.0\n\n# Requirements file references → copy the -r line as-is\npip install -r /Workspace/Shared/base-requirements.txt\n# → -r /Workspace/Shared/base-requirements.txt\n\n# DBFS requirements references → copy as-is\npip install -r /dbfs/cluster-init/requirements.txt\n# → -r /dbfs/cluster-init/requirements.txt\n\n# Index URL flags → preserve in output\npip install --index-url https://pypi.example.com/simple mypackage\n# → --index-url https://pypi.example.com/simple\n# → mypackage\n\npip install --extra-index-url https://pypi.example.com/simple mypackage\n# → --extra-index-url https://pypi.example.com/simple\n# → mypackage\n\n# Trusted host flags → preserve in output\npip install --trusted-host pypi.example.com mypackage\n# → --trusted-host pypi.example.com\n# → mypackage\n\n# Wheel file paths → copy path as-is\npip install /Workspace/Shared/libs/my_package-1.0-py3-none-any.whl\n# → /Workspace/Shared/libs/my_package-1.0-py3-none-any.whl\n\n# VCS URLs → copy as-is\npip install git+https://github.com/org/repo.git@v1.0\n# → git+https://github.com/org/repo.git@v1.0\n```\n\n**Ignore these pip flags** (they are install-time behavior, not dependency specs):\n\n- `--upgrade` / `-U`\n- `--force-reinstall`\n- `--no-cache-dir`\n- `--quiet` / `-q`\n- `--no-deps`\n- `--target` / `-t`\n- `--user`\n- `--prefix`\n\n### conda install commands\n\nExtract package names from conda install lines:\n\n```bash\n# Simple conda install → extract package names as pip requirements\nconda install numpy scipy matplotlib\n# → numpy\n# → scipy\n# → matplotlib\n\n# Conda with version pinning → translate to pip format\nconda install numpy=1.24.0 scipy>=1.10\n# → numpy==1.24.0   (note: conda uses single =, pip uses ==)\n# → scipy>=1.10\n\n# Conda with channel → ignore channel flag, just extract packages\nconda install -c conda-forge numpy\n# → numpy\n```\n\n### Translating conda package names to pip (supply chain safety)\n\nConda and PyPI are **separate registries**. The same name can refer to different packages on each, and a conda package may have no PyPI presence at all. Naively rewriting a conda name into `requirements.txt` risks installing the wrong package — or a malicious package that has squatted the name on PyPI — into the serverless environment, where it would run with the job\'s permissions.\n\n**Only auto-translate conda packages that appear in the short allowlists below. For anything else, pause and ask the user to confirm the PyPI name before writing the requirements.txt file.**\n\n#### Known conda → pip renames (auto-translate)\n\nThese are well-established packages whose conda name differs from their pip name:\n\n| Conda name   | Pip name        |\n| ------------ | --------------- |\n| `pytorch`    | `torch`         |\n| `pytables`   | `tables`        |\n| `py-xgboost` | `xgboost`       |\n| `py-opencv`  | `opencv-python` |\n\n#### Identical names safe to auto-translate\n\nThese packages have matching names on both registries and are maintained by the same trusted upstream projects:\n\n`numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `matplotlib`, `scikit-learn`, `seaborn`, `requests`, `pillow`, `jupyter`, `ipython`, `pyyaml`\n\n#### Everything else → ask the user\n\nIf a conda package is not on either allowlist, **do not silently write it to requirements.txt**. Pause and ask the user:\n\n> The init script installs `<conda-package>` via conda. Conda and PyPI are separate registries, so the same name may refer to a different package on PyPI, or this package may not exist on PyPI at all. To avoid installing the wrong package, please confirm the PyPI name (or paste the exact pip requirement line you want to use).\n\nUse the user\'s confirmed name in the output. If the user is unsure or cannot confirm, report the package as untranslatable rather than guessing.\n\n### Ignoring non-install flags\n\nSkip lines that are not library installation. Common patterns to ignore:\n\n- `conda install -y` — the `-y` flag is just auto-confirm, not a package\n- `conda install -n <env_name>` — the `-n` flag specifies a conda environment name, not a package\n- `pip install --upgrade pip` — upgrading pip itself is not needed in serverless environments\n- Lines with shell conditionals (`if`, `then`, `else`, `fi`) — parse only the pip/conda commands within them\n\n## Step 6: Deduplicate Within the Init Script\n\nIf the same package is installed more than once within a single init script, **keep only the last mention** and drop the earlier ones. This matches classic-compute runtime behavior: pip/conda install commands execute sequentially, so the last install of a given package reinstalls over anything earlier.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\npip install numpy==1.24.0\npip install numpy==1.25.0\n# → numpy==1.25.0  (the earlier pin is dropped)\n```\n\nDo not attempt to reconcile conflicts across multiple init scripts — each init script produces its own requirements.txt, and any cross-file conflicts are surfaced by pip at job runtime.\n\n---\n\n## Plan Output\n\nPhase 1 produces a translation plan. The plan must be **self-contained** (Phase 2 can execute from it alone without re-running analysis) and **easily parseable** (another agent or a human reviewer can read it and understand exactly what will be written and where).\n\nPer init script, include: source path, target filename, proposed requirements.txt contents (from Steps 5–6), and any untranslated operations flagged to the user.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Apply\n\n## Step 7: Write the Requirements File\n\nCreate the file at `Shared/serverless-migration/migrated-init-script-deps-<slug>.txt` using `createAsset`, then `editAsset` to write the contents. Intermediate directories are created automatically.\n\n`<slug>` is a unique-within-this-migration identifier derived from the source init script\'s path — e.g., replace `/` with `-` and strip the leading dash and `.sh` extension. For `/Shared/init-scripts/setup.sh` use `Shared-init-scripts-setup`. If two source paths produce the same slug, append `-1`, `-2`, etc.\n\nThe file should contain:\n\n1. A header comment identifying the source\n2. Any index URL or trusted host flags\n3. Any `-r` references (requirements file references from the original init script)\n4. Individual package requirements, one per line\n\n**Example output:**\n\n```txt\n# Auto-generated by serverless migration\n# Source init script: /Workspace/init-scripts/setup.sh\n# Generated: 2026-03-30\n\n--index-url https://pypi.example.com/simple\n--extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple\n\n-r /Workspace/Shared/base-requirements.txt\n\nrequests==2.31.0\nnumpy>=1.24.0\npandas\nscikit-learn==1.3.0\nxgboost\n```\n\n## Step 8: Reference in Job Config\n\nAdd the requirements file to the serverless job\'s `environments` spec. Each translated init script gets its own `-r` dependency line:\n\n```json\n{\n  "environments": [\n    {\n      "environment_key": "default",\n      "spec": {\n        "environment_version": "<environment_version>",\n        "dependencies": [\n          "-r \\"/Workspace/Shared/serverless-migration/migrated-init-script-deps-Shared-init-scripts-setup.txt\\"",\n          "-r \\"/Workspace/Shared/serverless-migration/migrated-init-script-deps-Shared-init-scripts-deps.txt\\""\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nEach `-r` line corresponds to exactly one source init script. Set `environment_version` from the source job\'s DBR version:\n\n| DBR Version | `environment_version` | Python |\n| ----------- | --------------------- | ------ |\n| 13.x, 14.x  | "1"                   | 3.10   |\n| 15.x        | "2"                   | 3.11   |\n| 16.x        | "3"                   | 3.12   |\n| 17.x        | "4"                   | 3.12   |\n| 18.x+       | "5"                   | 3.12   |\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n### Empty init scripts\n\nIf an init script has no library installation commands (only comments, echo statements, etc.), skip it — no requirements file needed.\n\n### pip install from git URLs\n\nGit URLs like `git+https://github.com/org/repo.git@v1.0#egg=package` are valid pip requirements lines. Include them as-is in the requirements.txt.\n\n### Workspace-level (global) init scripts\n\nNot part of the job\'s cluster spec and not run on serverless. If the workspace has any, ask the user whether the job depends on their side effects.\n'}}]);
